          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }}
>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Interior Painting Timeline</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Interior Painting Timeline in Occupied Homes</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        How long it takes, paint odor concerns, and staying in your home during interior painting in humid Central Florida.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            Painting the interior of an occupied home in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a>, or <a href="/areas/central-florida" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Central Florida</a> is common—most homeowners don't move out during the project. But how long does it take, can you sleep in a freshly painted room, and what about paint odor? Here's what to expect based on 15+ years of <a href="/services/interior-exterior-painting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">interior painting</a> in lived-in Florida homes.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">How Long Does Interior Painting Take?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>Single room (bedroom, bathroom):</strong> 1-2 days<br />
            <strong>Living room or main bedroom:</strong> 2-3 days<br />
            <strong>Whole house interior (2,000 sq ft, 3BR/2BA):</strong> 5-7 days<br />
            <strong>Large home (3,000+ sq ft, 4+BR):</strong> 7-10 days
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Timeline depends on room count, ceiling height, trim and door count, and how much furniture needs moving. Rooms painted one at a time so you can still live in the house—we paint bedrooms first, then living areas, then common spaces.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Day-by-Day Timeline for a Typical Room</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Day 1: Prep & First Coat</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Move furniture to center of room, cover with drop cloths</li>
            <li>Tape baseboards, trim, ceiling edges</li>
            <li>Patch holes and cracks with spackle, sand smooth</li>
            <li>Wipe down walls to remove dust</li>
            <li>Cut in edges (brush corners, trim, ceiling line)</li>
            <li>Roll first coat on walls</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            First coat dries in 2-4 hours in normal conditions. In Central Florida's humid summers, it can take 4-6 hours. We ventilate with fans and open windows (if humidity allows).
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Day 2: Second Coat & Touch-Ups</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Apply second coat (walls usually need two coats for even coverage)</li>
            <li>Paint trim and doors if included</li>
            <li>Touch up any missed spots</li>
            <li>Remove tape carefully while paint is still slightly wet</li>
            <li>Clean up, move furniture back</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Second coat dries to touch in 2-4 hours, fully cures in 24-48 hours. You can move furniture back the same day, but avoid leaning items against freshly painted walls for 24 hours.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Can You Sleep in a Freshly Painted Room?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>Low-VOC or zero-VOC paint (what we use):</strong> Yes, same night. Low-VOC paint has minimal odor and off-gassing. Open a window or run a fan for ventilation, but it's safe to sleep in the room once the paint is dry to touch (2-4 hours).
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>Traditional oil-based or high-VOC paint:</strong> Wait 24-48 hours. These paints have stronger fumes that can cause headaches or irritation.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most Kissimmee and Orlando homeowners choose low-VOC paint specifically so they can stay in the house during painting. It costs the same as regular paint and has almost no smell.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Dealing with Paint Odor in Florida Humidity</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            Central Florida humidity slows paint drying, which can prolong paint odor. Here's how to ventilate and speed up drying:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Run fans:</strong> Circulate air with box fans or ceiling fans (not pointed directly at wet paint).</li>
            <li><strong>Open windows:</strong> Only if outdoor humidity is lower than indoors. In humid months (June-September), AC with closed windows often works better.</li>
            <li><strong>Use AC:</strong> Air conditioning dehumidifies and speeds drying. Keep temperature consistent (72-78°F).</li>
            <li><strong>Avoid painting on very humid days:</strong> Humidity over 85% significantly slows drying.</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Our take:</strong> We schedule interior painting during Florida's drier months (October-May) when possible, and always use low-VOC paint so families can stay in the house comfortably. We paint rooms sequentially—finish one room, let it air out, move to the next. Most clients barely notice the smell.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Living in the House During Painting</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>We paint one or two rooms at a time</strong> so the rest of the house stays usable. Furniture from the room being painted moves to another room temporarily. Kitchens and bathrooms are painted fast (1 day each) to minimize disruption.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>Kids and pets:</strong> Keep them out of rooms being painted. Once paint is dry to touch (2-4 hours), the room is safe. Low-VOC paint is non-toxic once dry.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Furniture protection:</strong> We cover everything with drop cloths and plastic. Floors are protected with canvas drop cloths (not plastic, which is slippery).
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Florida-Specific Painting Considerations</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Humidity slows drying:</strong> Paint takes 1.5-2x longer to dry in humid months. Plan extra time between coats.</li>
            <li><strong>Mold-resistant paint:</strong> We use mold-resistant primer and paint in Florida homes to prevent mildew in bathrooms, kitchens, and humid rooms.</li>
            <li><strong>Ventilation:</strong> Closed-up Florida homes with AC running can trap paint fumes longer than homes with open windows in dry climates. We use fans and ventilate strategically.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Common Questions</h2>
          
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">How long does paint smell last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                Low-VOC paint: light smell for a few hours, gone within 24 hours. Traditional paint: noticeable smell for 1-3 days, faint odor for a week. Ventilation speeds it up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">Can we stay in the house while you paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                Yes. We paint rooms one at a time so the house stays livable. Use low-VOC paint to minimize odor. Most families go about their normal routines—just avoid the room being painted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">How long until I can put furniture back?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                Same day once paint is dry to touch (2-4 hours). Avoid pushing heavy furniture against walls for 24 hours to prevent scuffing or sticking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">What if I have kids or pets?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                Keep them out of the room being painted. Once paint is dry (2-4 hours), it's safe. Low-VOC paint is non-toxic once cured. We work fast to minimize disruption.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Interior painting in an occupied Kissimmee, Orlando, or Central Florida home typically takes 1-2 days per room. Use low-VOC paint to minimize odor. You can sleep in a freshly painted room the same night. Humidity slows drying, so plan extra time in summer months. Most families stay in the house during painting with no issues—we work room by room, ventilate well, and use low-smell paint.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Need Your Home Painted?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
              We paint occupied homes in Kissimmee, Orlando, and Central Florida. Low-VOC paint, fast timelines, minimal disruption. Free estimates.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-sans">
              <a href="/guides" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">← Back to All Guides</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection
    title="Interior Painting for Occupied Homes"
    description="Low-VOC paint, minimal odor, room-by-room approach. Serving Kissimmee, Orlando & Central Florida."
  />

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
